{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB09351 - MONDO_6875",
  "verbalization": "Levobetaxolol, a beta-blocker, is implicated in the pathogenesis of ocular hypertension through its indirect effects on intraocular pressure. The evidence suggests that Levobetaxolol may be repurposed for ocular hypertension via its role in the treatment of open-angle glaucoma, a condition associated with elevated intraocular pressure. This is supported by the indication of Levobetaxolol in open-angle glaucoma, which in turn is linked to latanoprost and befunolol, both of which are used in the management of ocular hypertension. The shared pathway from Levobetaxolol to open-angle glaucoma and then to ocular hypertension indicates a potential mechanistic link, particularly through the influence of these drugs on intraocular pressure regulation. The final scores suggest a moderate level of confidence in this relationship, though further investigation is needed to confirm the direct mechanism of action and to assess the clinical relevance of this repurposing.",
